            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's core identification relies on Ezekiel 27, where Scripture explicitly defines a ship by mapping its parts to a city, its rulers to pilots, and its inhabitants to a crew. The common interpretation treats the Gospel ship as the 'Church' and the prophetic ship as 'commerce' or 'pride,' fracturing the symbol. The book unifies the symbol by defining a ship structurally as an organized polity, city, or state riding upon a sea of peoples. This allows Tyre, Babylon, and the governed company of disciples to share the exact same structural symbol. The secondary assertion that Christ rules the waters without a vessel but acts as Head when boarding one elegantly explains the distinction between His walking on water (inherent rule over peoples) and His sailing with the disciples.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"An organized kingdom, city, or state riding upon the sea of peoples.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Its rulers steer and inhabitants crew it\",\n    \"Christ can rule the waters without a vessel, but when He boards one it comes under its true Head\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The identification of Jonah's ship specifically as Jerusalem/Judea and the typological parallel to Caiaphas.\",\n    \"The specific allegorical reading of Acts 27 where the ship represents a state perishing while its people are preserved.\",\n    \"The argument excluding Noah's ark from the ship symbol based on the Hebrew word 'tevah'.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The ship is not a universal, fixed symbol but a context-dependent figure. In Ezekiel 27, it represents Tyre specifically because Tyre was a literal island maritime power, making a ship an apt poetic metaphor for that specific city. In the Gospels and Acts, boats are literal historical objects that early Christians later allegorized into the Church. In Revelation, ships represent literal maritime trade mourning the economic fall of an empire.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A literal maritime vessel used locally as a metaphor for maritime commerce or functioning as a historical setting, lacking a single unified symbolic referent.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"Denies that 'ship' universally or fundamentally symbolizes a governed polity or state across Scripture.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Tyre was an actual island city renowned for its sea trade, making Ezekiel's allegory a natural historical metaphor for that specific target rather than a universal symbolic code.\",\n    \"The boats in the Gospels and Acts are literal vessels used for literal transportation and fishing, not symbolic constructs introduced to represent states.\",\n    \"The traditional Church-as-ship reading derives from narrative application (safety in a storm) and typology (Noah's ark), not from Ezekiel's political mapping.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Provides a unified symbolic definition that applies structurally to Ezekiel's Tyre, John's Babylon, and the Gospel narratives, whereas the counter-interpretation fractures the meaning based on genre or historical context.\",\n    \"Accounts for the precise internal mapping in Ezekiel 27 (pilots = wise men/rulers; mariners = inhabitants), which flawlessly matches the mechanics of a governed state.\",\n    \"Explains the narrative contrast in the Gospels of Christ walking natively on the sea (direct rule over the sea of peoples) versus boarding a ship (taking the headship of an organized, governed company).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Does not require reading every ordinary boat in a historical narrative (such as the fishing boats in Luke 5 or the Alexandrian grain ship in Acts 27) as a governed polity.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Ezekiel 27:8\",\n    \"Ezekiel 27:27\",\n    \"Revelation 18:17-19\",\n    \"Matthew 14:25-33\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
